editorial:
Let pi[i] be the length of the longest proper prefix of s[0..i] that is also a suffix. If pi[n−1] = b, the first b characters match the last b. Thus shifting by p = n−b matches every overlapping character. Any smaller positive shift would give a longer matching prefix and suffix, so p is the smallest period.

A period can leave an incomplete final block. For abababa the smallest period is 2, but 2 does not divide 7. Why can we then return n? If the string were two or more copies of a block of length d, shifting its repeating block by p would preserve every character (there are at least two copies and p ≤ d). Positions in that block spaced by gcd(d,p) would therefore agree. Minimality of p forces gcd(d,p) = p, so p divides d and hence n.

Build pi left to right. On a mismatch, fall back to the next shorter matching prefix using pi[j−1]. Each fallback decreases j, and j increases at most once per character, giving O(n) time and O(n) extra space.
~~~
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::string s;
    std::cin >> s;
    const int n = static_cast<int>(s.size());
    std::vector<int> pi(n);
    for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
        int j = pi[i - 1];
        while (j > 0 && s[i] != s[j]) j = pi[j - 1];
        if (s[i] == s[j]) ++j;
        pi[i] = j;
    }
    const int p = n - pi.back();
    std::cout << (n % p == 0 ? p : n) << '\n';
}
~~~
